===Etymology===
{{bor|en|frm|rapiere}}, from {{der|en|frm|([[espee]]) [[rapiere]]}}, from {{der|en|fro|rapiere}}, {{m|fro|raspiere}}, from {{der|en|es|raspadera|t=poker; raker; scraper}}, from {{der|en|es|raspar|t=to scrape}}, of {{etyl|gem|en}} origin. More at {{l|en|rasp}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/ˈɹeɪpˌɪə(ɹ)/|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[slender]], [[straight]], [[sharp]]ly [[pointed]] [[sword]] (double-[[edge]]d, single-edged or edgeless).
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Hamlet}}, act IV scene 1
#*: […] In his lawless fit,
#*: Behind the arras hearing something stir,
#*: Whips out his '''rapier''', cries ‘A rat, a rat!’
#*: And in this brainish apprehension kills
#*: The unseen good old man.

